WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:05.260
Right, this is going to be a slightly more improvised
video than normal, for two reasons.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.420
Er, one, it is really cold here
and I want to move on,

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.760
but also because I didn’t know I was
going to be passing through Schengen,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.970
a little town in Luxembourg on the borders
with France and Germany, until today.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.330
But here I am, next to the
international border, the Moselle River,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.380
and that border is a little bit weird.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.960
And I wasn’t going to do another video about
a weird border. Because I’ve done that.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.419
Years ago I went to the towns of
Baarle-Nassau and Baarle-Hertog

00:00:26.419 --> 00:00:27.650
in Belgium and the Netherlands,

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.169
where the borders are incredibly complicated
and twisted thanks to history.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:34.660
But that’s an old video that
I’m not really proud of,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.090
and frankly it’s not that interesting:

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.960
I only managed about a minute of script

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.850
before I had to go into a diversion on sovereignty

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.370
and why we even have borders
in the first place.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.870
Strange borders often make
for great video titles,

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.820
but there’s not much to talk about
other than “huh, that’s weird”.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.500
Which is also why I’ve never been to the island
that changes country every six months

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.730
between France and Spain.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:01.280
Because once you’re there, what is
there to say but, “huh, that’s weird”.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.070
But it turns out this border
raises a different question.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:07.280
If you look on the map, there is a tripoint
just of there, to the west of an island:

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.220
that’s where France, Germany and Luxembourg
all meet.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.900
It was here – actually, on a boat out at
that tripoint,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.870
where the Schengen agreement was signed,

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.452
where most of the countries of the EU,
all the ones that have their flags here,

00:01:18.452 --> 00:01:20.180
decided to open their internal borders.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.420
But that the tripoint there
is not strictly true.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.780
The border between Germany and Luxembourg
doesn’t follow the Moselle river:

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.650
the border is the Moselle river.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.110
And that’s an important distinction.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.350
In most cases, international borders that
follow rivers

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.799
are defined as following the centre line,

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:37.290
which raises questions in itself,

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.890
because rivers can erode the banks and change
paths sometimes,

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:45.280
but borders are, at least, usually defined
as a line of theoretically zero width.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.970
Which is how mapmakers mark it.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:52.330
But here, Germany and Luxembourg share the
river north of France. It is a "condominium".

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.869
Under the treaties they signed,
they both have full control over it.

00:01:55.869 --> 00:01:57.960
Which means that if you’re standing on the
north tip of that island,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.350
or on that bridge up there,

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.450
you are in both countries at the same time.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.030
Not just in terms of
having a foot on each side,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.740
although that may well be
how the locals treat it,

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.890
and that’s certainly how
the signs mark it.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:09.580
But no: standing on that bridge
above the water,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.360
all of you is in both countries simultaneously.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.880
This international border is a shape,
not a line. It’s two-dimensional.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.220
Although, actually, I guess it’s
three-dimensional if you count depth.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.569
Half an hour of research, and I can’t tell you
why they made that decision,

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:27.100
or what the legal result would be if you tried
to commit a crime

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.940
on that bridge or on a boat on the river.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:31.370
I even asked the folks at the little
European museum just over there,

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.700
and they didn’t know the answer.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.170
Although they do have a lovely little display
of all the border guard hats

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.070
that the Schengen agreement made obsolete.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:45.230
I suspect that the answer would be
“treat it as if it’s a line, it’s easier”.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.310
And that’s what I mean when I say that there’s
not much to talk about here.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.560
Because that’s one of the wonderful things
about borders like this:

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.500
when you can walk between three countries

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.360
as easily as you can walk between
counties or towns or neighbourhoods:

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.040
it’s a lot easier to say “huh, that’s weird”

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.760
and just get on with your life.

